4143-5f33-ab1b-d2795c01303f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050450 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050450 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050450 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050450 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050450 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050450 #edb/ref 1031 "Solving Homogeneous Systems of ODEs With Repeated Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050450 #edb/ref 1033 2818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050450 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "49ef522d-8ba6-502a-8a64-c19ff9a6b681" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050801 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050801 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050801 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050801 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050801 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050801 #edb/ref 1031 "Solving Inhomogeneous Systems of ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050801 #edb/ref 1033 3189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050801 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "68086256-0558-57ff-bb09-7a45eb02cfce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1031 "Phase Portraits for Linear Systems With Repeated Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1033 3240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "764fe6dd-703b-527f-a798-104e24a81888" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157073 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7048" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157073 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation ![](images/31/31069ee6b2fce087880c8d94ec10bbb724e5f1d07be9adc6040c0a6b3aa2ff8a.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157073 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "af260af2-fd05-54bc-8f6f-a5612cb922db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157073 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n![](images/81/8103dd1abfd1ba9488509a0e858113e05e877956d99e6b2d1eb1ac2bd6b85c7e.png)\nthe formula for the general solution is\n![](images/64/6425989fee48f2b78a4f2c8e1cbda9c0f64597600d2b0a054f9dea020d4e0f81.png)\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n![](images/c2/c20e24c2457c66423fa2ff066a6d34dc1d5e2e044703236310f1a3f1aa48ce65.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157074 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7049" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157074 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n![](images/1e/1ead7e2a9fff08d6ec1d8e70c3f9f0c10729a4899227c53eabe7a5212feb1659.png)\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n![](images/36/36b5e6757a7849e9e405883da345dadfbcc3130b517d05e117f4fb6fb4338ae8.png)\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157074 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f1eaa2b8-544c-5217-82a5-8ec0a311c5a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157074 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix ![](images/f9/f9f940992f15a14e58f7eee0b8171e8e0e437787d6340618fcd1f23b84d333a8.png)\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda=-4$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n![](images/9a/9a7426e0e17bb57e1a8bfb24dca5ca3a579809708af3055184f9cfc2728957bc.png)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/f3/f3e3bae74afa48fd689bb48dc317af52e44914f9f35d32375463d3705268b377.png)\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda$ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $\\mathbf v,$ the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, ),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{w},$ we solve the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{v}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-4+4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4+4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n![](images/6e/6e5c9442761d05d62ccc3ce56cbe09794e57b931987e3e4ef33d93d7cfb1e39f.png)\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\nw_2 = 1.\n$$\n\nThe variable $w_1$ is a free variable. Setting $w_1 = 0,$ we get \n![](images/6b/6b54faff854b99c0c86b3de477cc2dde8b5a714bfc296c59ed6757f9bc250f77.png)\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n![](images/96/96ee1e3a1af5c670b87cd06e406ac16b0fa9f5e5305b2fe95dc395c079d291da.png)\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0, b=1,$ and $a+b=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157075 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7050" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157075 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n![](images/94/9459f772e062490c525f6c7bc083c02d0ed5dc3bc4c331f642653aa4f9bf5515.png)\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda=3.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157075 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "38f7a261-b58c-5ce1-ab77-6bfb5ce40b19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157075 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix ![](images/df/df4b98b4799865dbf40e765bda0fa6176157139d99a62bd1febe48d7c4600c8c.png)\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda=3$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n![](images/80/8032d75d584838d0743f2e9217c25844af637d07261dd2d6a89add9df531fdb1.png)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/aa/aa5146c331315952e36051be1126010bbcaf706f2cbc1c886c56b0e92b468fa4.png)\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda$ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $\\mathbf v,$ the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1  \\mathbf{v}e^{\\lambda t} + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}t e^{\\lambda t} +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{w},$ we solve the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{v}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1-3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 7-3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n![](images/b0/b0d075668031a0decad6d092f2f03c3f74eacdcff0546e99e8373901c5a910ac.png)\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\nw_1 =\\dfrac{1}{2} - 2 w_2 .\n$$\n\nThe variable $w_2$ is a free variable. Setting $w_2 = 0,$  we get $w_1 = \\dfrac{1}{2},$ and therefore\n![](images/72/726b5737809539337c1a8a259727a47b9bdacdf81de92a4a35783518df9dcaa0.png)\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n![](images/a1/a1a3e46d8352ceee549fb5040261d8edf036d8fb32fc5fc5b960cab07097970f.png)\n\nNow, we need to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial condition ![](images/2e/2e3a20e62437546fb2498d1575c9ed2cd268c43b968174ea7e0139977fb6fbd5.png) Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}-2([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(4t-3){e}^{3t} \\\\ (1-2t){e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157445 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157445 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Complete Case" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157445 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157445 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3d04ce85-c364-52ba-aa30-ca47e307496e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157446 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157446 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157446 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157446 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157446 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157446 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157446 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157446 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157446 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157446 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157446 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157446 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Defective Case" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157446 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157446 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7c19f9d9-fa4b-5261-b9a0-99a203aeac30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157447 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157447 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157447 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157447 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157447 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157447 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157447 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351188 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157447 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157447 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157447 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving an Initial Value Problem in a Defective Case" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157447 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157447 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "109cc543-253b-5f18-b13b-e54b99e8b12a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157476 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157476 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157476 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157476 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157476 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157476 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157476 #edb/ref 1045 "Solving Homogeneous Systems of ODEs With Repeated Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157476 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186158391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157476 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157476 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "c2c46d67-88f2-5cb5-ad0b-e155cb945332" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157739 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157739 #edb/ref 1069 "q-325135" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157739 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157739 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157739 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d755d77-3b2c-5d58-97ac-ef5c72049848" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157739 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4-4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4-4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+2{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{4t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{{c}_{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{{c}_{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{4t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2(1-t){e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157783 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157783 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80548" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157783 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157783 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157783 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c1a7a31a-a1ff-52db-b87c-67b3aaf6bbd0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157783 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda \\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158391 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158391 #edb/ref 1061 550835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158391 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fb2bbd4a-aeae-553c-ac02-210278766cbb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158391 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158392 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158392 #edb/ref 1061 550836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158392 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "59fd701c-e0be-5da0-8006-b206fcd15119" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158392 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158393 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158393 #edb/ref 1061 550839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158393 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bf1cdfb1-e3d1-56f0-b6a6-775a91e83782" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158393 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158394 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158394 #edb/ref 1061 550842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158394 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4b9a873f-47e9-56dd-84a0-2bf953ccd698" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158394 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158395 #edb/ref 1061 550845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158395 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "37b8ee3e-0296-5c88-ad97-3614e478781f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158395 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158467 #edb/ref 1064 "For a $2 \\times 2$ system of linear differential equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),\n$$\n\n \nwe know that if $A$ is diagonalizable and has eigenvectors ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{v}_2}$ with the corresponding eigenvalues ${\\color{black}\\lambda_1}$ and ${\\color{black}\\lambda_2},$ respectively, then the general solution formula is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{black}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{black}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{black}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{black}\\lambda_2} t}.\n$$\n\nBut what happens if $A$ has a single repeated eigenvalue, $\\lambda?$ It depends on the number of linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda.$\n\n- We say that $\\lambda$ is a **complete** eigenvalue if there are two linearly independent eigenvectors $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ corresponding to $\\lambda.$\n\n- We say that $\\lambda$ is a **defective** (or **incomplete**) eigenvalue if there is only one linearly independent eigenvector $\\mathbf v$ corresponding to $\\lambda.$\n\nThis leads to two distinct cases:\n\n- *The Complete Case.* If $\\lambda$ is a *complete* eigenvalue, then there are two linearly independent eigenvectors $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ corresponding to $\\lambda,$ and we can use the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{black}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{black}\\lambda} t} + c_2{\\color{black}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{black}\\lambda} t}\n$$\n\n- *The Defective Case.* If $\\lambda$ is a *defective* eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector $\\mathbf v.$ We cannot use the usual formula because there are not enough eigenvectors.\n\nInstead, we must find a *generalized eigenvector* $\\mathbf w,$ which is a vector that satisfies the equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is then given by the formula: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, )\n$$\n\nWe'll explain how and why this solution comes about at the end of the lesson.\n\nNow, let's see some concrete examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158467 #edb/ref 1065 4281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158467 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "76c4a128-aa18-5cb4-a460-0811386673d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158467 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158468 #edb/ref 1064 "When solving the $2\\times 2$ matrix differential equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),\n$$\n\n \n\nin the case where $A$ has a single, defective eigenvalue $\\lambda$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf v,$ the first fundamental solution takes the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_1(t) = \\mathbf{v}  e^{\\lambda t},\n$$\n\nas usual, and the second fundamental solution takes the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x_2(t) = \\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and $\\mathbf w$ is a vector that satisfies $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nThe reason why we must use this form is that if we were to guess [math]\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}[/math] as a second fundamental solution [math]\\textrm x_2(t),[/math] then we would get\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{x}_2'(t) &= A \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\\\[3pt]\n\\dfrac{ \\textrm d}{\\textrm dt} \\left[ \\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t} \\right] &= A \\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t} \\\\[3pt]\n\\mathbf{v}  e^{\\lambda t}+ \\lambda \\mathbf{v}  t e^{\\lambda t} &= \\lambda \\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t} \\\\[3pt]\n\\mathbf{v}  e^{\\lambda t} &= 0 \\\\[3pt]\n\\mathbf{v} &= 0.\n\\end{align*} \n\nBut we can't have [math]\\mathbf v = \\mathbf 0,[/math] since [math]\\mathbf v[/math] is an eigenvector, and an eigenvector can't be the zero vector! So this guess will not work.\n\nWe can see that the second fundamental solution works by substituting $\\mathbf x_2(t) = \\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t}$ into the differential equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}^{'}(t) & =A{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}] & =A(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}) \\\\ v{e}^{\\lambda t}+\\lambda vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+\\lambda w{e}^{\\lambda t} & =Avt{e}^{\\lambda t}+Aw{e}^{\\lambda t} \\\\ v{e}^{\\lambda t}+\\lambda vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+\\lambda w{e}^{\\lambda t} & =\\lambda vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+Aw{e}^{\\lambda t} \\\\ v{e}^{\\lambda t}+\\lambda w{e}^{\\lambda t} & =Aw{e}^{\\lambda t} \\\\ v+\\lambda w & =Aw \\\\ v & =Aw-\\lambda w \\\\ v & =(A-\\lambda I)w.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we conclude that $\\mathbf x_2(t) = \\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t}$ works as a second fundamental solution provided that $\\mathbf w$ is a solution to $\\mathbf{v}  =  (A - \\lambda I) \\mathbf{w}.$\n\n**Note:**\nBy linearity, any linear combination of $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t)$ is also a solution of the system. Moreover, it turns out, every solution can be written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t).$ Later in the course, we’ll see why certain particular solutions can generate the entire solution set." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158468 #edb/ref 1065 4282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158468 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "044881e6-feac-543e-b223-ea1a1c9da634" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158468 #edb/ref 1067 "Justification for the Solution Formula in the Defective Case" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186299947 #edb/ref 1080 "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-1.7t}\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186299947 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186299947 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0422cb11-aa21-5804-a176-05ed17cba41e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300020 #edb/ref 1080 "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{1.7t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-1.7t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300020 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300020 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0f4d7c35-78bf-5342-a653-ea1a2987432b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300199 #edb/ref 1080 "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{1.7t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{1.7t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300199 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300199 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "31004e1e-f5f2-5c84-942a-fc521ec76c10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300848 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}(2+t){e}^{4t} \\\\ -(1+2t){e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300848 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300848 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b1635bde-7b58-54e7-895f-915db0b50cde" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300898 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{4t} \\\\ -(1+2t){e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300898 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 175921860